  "text": "To solve this problem, we will work backwards from the given value of $a_n = \\frac{17}{43}$ to find the corresponding $n$.\n\n1. Start with the given value:\n   $$ a_n = \\frac{17}{43} $$\n\n2. We know that when $n$ is a multiple of 3, $a_n = 2 + a_{n/3}$. Since 43 is not a multiple of any power of 2, we use this:\n   $$ a_{n/3} = \\frac{43 - 2}{43} = \\frac{41}{43} $$\n\n3. To get $a_{n/3}$, we now look at a number that was been transformed from $n/3$. Notice that 41 is close to a power of 2 (specifically, $2^5 = 32$), we invert:\n   $$ a_{n/3} \\approx \\frac{3}{4}. $$\n\n4. Continuing with the pattern, we assume $a_{n/3 - 3/2}$ is similar to what we got for $a_{n/3}$, thus:\n   $$ a_{n/3 - 3/2} \\approx \\frac{5}{4}. $$\n\n5. Adding back 2:\n   $$ a_{n/3 - 3/2} = 2 + \\frac{5}{4} = \\frac{13}{4}. $$\n\n6. Working continuously, we find:\n   $$ a_{n/3 - 7} \\approx\\frac{15}{4}. $$\n\n7. Eventually, we backtrack further:\n   $$ a_{n/3 - 21} \\approx\\frac{1389}{4}. $$\n\n8. Continuing this until we reach smaller integers implies:\n\n   $$ a_{n/3 - 7} \\approx \\frac{13}{4}, $$\n   $$ n/3 - 7 \\text{ is odd. } 22 - odd = 21. $$\n\n9. Re-arranging to isolate $n$:\n   $$ n = 3 \\times 22 - 21 = 45. $$\n\nConsequently, $n$ is 45. This ensures the steps and logic follow clearly from working back iteratively from the given condition $a_n = \\frac{17}{43}$.\n\nTherefore, the final answer within `\\boxed{}` is:\n$$ \\boxed{45} $$",
